01:07:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Excellent question! It really gets at a common confusion about what a “sonic boom” actually is. The short answer is: **A sonic boom isn’t caused by speed alone—it’s caused by a physical object shoving air molecules out of its way faster than they can naturally get out of the way.** Light and radio waves are not physical objects; they don’t push air aside, so they can’t create a sonic boom. Let’s break that down. ### What really causes a sonic boom? Imagine a bullet, a jet, or a whip cracking. These are solid (or liquid) objects moving through air. As they move, they push air molecules aside. Normally, the air politely moves out of the way in smooth waves of pressure—those are just ordinary sound waves. But when the object moves faster than the speed of sound (about 343 m/s in air), it’s outrunning its own pressure waves. The air can’t get out of the way in time, so the waves pile up on top of each other and form a single, intense shock wave. That sudden pressure spike is what we hear as a “sonic boom.” **Crucial point:** The object has to be **physically colliding with and displacing air molecules** to create that chain reaction. ### Why don’t light and radio waves do that? Light (visible light, radio waves, X-rays, etc.) is **electromagnetic radiation**, not a stream of “light particles” in the sense of tiny bullets of matter. They are oscillating electric and magnetic fields that travel through space. They don’t need air to move, and they don’t interact with air by pushing it aside the way a baseball does. - **A photon** (a particle of light) has no electric charge and almost no interaction with neutral air molecules that would cause it to shove them out of the way. It might occasionally scatter off an air molecule, but it doesn’t bulldoze a path through the air. - **Radio waves** are also photons, just with much lower energy. They pass through air with almost zero effect. - Since they don’t displace air, there are no pressure waves building up, so no shock wave, and therefore no sonic boom. ### The lamp analogy When you flip a light switch, the light spreads instantaneously (from our perspective) into the room. Photons zoom out at about 300,000,000 m/s through air—nearly a million times faster than sound. If you wave your hand through the air that fast, you’d certainly cause a massive sonic boom (and vaporize the room). But the light itself doesn’t shove air; it just illuminates it. **Think of it this way:** You can’t cause a sonic boom by shouting “BOOM!” even though your voice travels at the speed of sound. The sound waves _are_ the pressure waves. They don’t break the sound barrier because they _are_ sound, not an object overtaking sound. Similarly, light is its own kind of wave, not an object trying to break the sound barrier. ### Fun extra: light can create pressure (but not a boom) Light does carry momentum, and when it hits a surface, it exerts a tiny pressure—called **radiation pressure**. But this pressure is so minuscule in everyday life that you never hear it. Solar sails in space use this to push spacecraft, but in air, the effect is negligible and doesn’t involve shock waves. Again, no boom. So to sum up: speed alone doesn’t make a sonic boom; you need a massive object that pushes air out of the way faster than sound can. Light and radio waves are massless waves of electric and magnetic fields, not air-pushers, so they zip along silently no matter how fast they go.
