oom is **a physical displacement of air that happens faster than the air can get out of the way.** That displacement can be caused by: - A solid object plowing through air (jet, bullet, whip). - A sudden thermal expansion of a gas (lightning, an explosion, a volcanic eruption). - Even the rapid collapse of a void (cavitation in water, but that's a different fluid). In every case, matter (usually air itself) is being violently pushed aside, and that push creates the shock wave. ### But what about light? Why doesn't the light from a lightning bolt make a boom? The lightning bolt emits light, of course—that's the flash you see. But that light, those photons, don’t heat the bulk air; they just travel through it. They’re the very same kind of electromagnetic wave as the light from a lamp, only brighter. They cause an instantaneous flash, but they don't physically shove the air molecules. The heating that makes the boom comes from the **electrical current**, not the light. So the lamp in your room is silent because its light doesn't dump energy into the air in a way that creates a rapid pressure wave (it just gently warms the air a tiny bit over time). Lightning’s light is silent for the same reason—it’s the electrical discharge’s energy vaporizing the air channel that causes the boom, not the light itself. ### The real takeaway The “sound barrier” isn’t about speed as a number on a dial. It’s about **whether there’s a physical mechanism that forces air molecules to move outward in a shock wave.** Jets and whips do it mechanically. Lightning does it thermally. Light and radio waves do neither; they pass through without forcing air molecules into a panic. So your lightning example is a perfect test case. It shows that you don't need a solid object, but you absolutely do need a violent shove on the air itself. Light simply doesn't shove. Does that clarify the distinction? It’s a subtle one, and you’re asking exactly the right questions to get to the bottom of it.
